     "type": "application",
     "title": "Draft a Mini-Plan Using the Framework",
     "content": "Choose one real project or goal you are currently responsible for, even a modest one - a school assignment with several parts, a household project, a small work initiative. Using the sequence from this lesson, write brief answers to each: State the mission in one sentence, distinct from any single method of achieving it. List the actual resources available to you - time, help, budget, tools - as an honest inventory. Name at least two constraints you cannot change, that the plan must work around rather than wish away. If anyone else is involved, identify one portion of the plan you could hand to them to design themselves, rather than dictating it. Identify the single most likely point of failure in the plan, and write one sentence describing your fallback if it happens. Finally, note one person besides yourself who should hear the entire plan, not just their own piece of it, before work begins. When you finish, compare this to how you would have planned the same project without the framework - most people, working from memory, skip the constraints step and the contingency step first. Notice which of the six answers was hardest to write; that is usually the step your planning habits skip by default.",
