     "content": "Pick a real or realistic organizational failure - one you have witnessed, read about, or can construct plausibly from a workplace, school, or team you know. Write a short description of the failure in neutral terms, without yet assigning fault. Now make two separate lists. In the first list, identify any decisions or behaviors within the failure that were genuinely within the control of an individual leader close to the situation - things they could have done differently with the authority they actually held. In the second list, identify any factors that were set by people or forces above or outside that leader's authority - budget levels, staffing ratios, policies, deadlines, or incentive structures the leader did not control. Look at your two lists and ask which one is longer, and which one the organization is more likely to focus on when assigning blame after the fact, since individual blame is generally simpler to communicate than structural reform. Finally, write one sentence describing what would have to change structurally, independent of any individual's effort or ownership, for this failure to be meaningfully less likely next time. This exercise does not ask you to excuse individuals who really did have the authority and failed to use it - it asks you to build the habit of checking whether the authority was actually there before accepting or assigning ownership language, which is the discipline the critique in this lesson is asking you to add to the framework, not subtract from it.",
